- 2026-10-29 06:14 UTC: The spike is done (its report and fixtures are in `.internal/`): Claude Code 2.1.308, Codex 0.162.1 and OpenCode 2.1.4 each ran its TUI as `lane-1` under the broker and the GPU fence, in a pseudo-terminal (`node-pty` 1.1.0) with `@xterm/headless` 6.0.0, against a scripted stand-in model, and none left a screen waiting once seeded; every decision can come from the session log, the hooks or OpenCode's server, never the screen. Found: Claude Code's first message must follow `--`; its token on file descriptor 3 needs the host to spawn the program on a pseudo-terminal pair itself (`setsid -c`), since `node-pty`'s spawn passes no descriptor; its transcript gives a usage limit's reset as a number (`resetsAt`, `rateLimitType`) and records typed text taken in mid-turn; its hooks (Stop's block, PostToolUse's context) steer in the TUI; it has a messaging socket the review did not know, left closed. Codex's rollout is written line by line, its hooks match Claude Code's, and an interrupt leaves its command running. OpenCode's session-log route gives nothing usable at 2.1.4: its event stream (`/api/event`, or the plugin) and its messages route are the record, its prompt route steers or queues, and its server outlives the TUI. The TUI costs 170 to 300 MiB a program (OpenCode's server and TUI about 450), the host 60 to 100 MiB; the Pi view (`pi-tui` 1.4.0) 60 MiB. Offline, Claude Code's TUI sends the same requests as `-p` but 1.4% larger, plus a title call per typed prompt. Next: the session-log adapters, beside today's stream readers, with the spike's logs as fixtures.
