- 2026-10-31 06:32 UTC: On a Kubernetes cluster, the supervisor's share of a rig's memory follows how its agent programs run, as the owner set: half a CPU and 4 GiB in their own terminal, 2 GiB headless, the harness taking the rest (at least 1 CPU and 4 GiB). So such a rig needs 1.5 CPUs and 8 GiB, and less is refused in words when the rig is added, resized, updated or switched to terminal. Control reads the mode from the rig's report each time it writes the deployments (a new rig: terminal) and records it in the rig's spec; a rig set up earlier keeps its 2 GiB until its next size change or image update. A new mode from Edit rig, REST or MCP on a rig with its CPUs and memory set changes its size as `set_cluster_rig_resources` does: checked first, then the setting sent, then the pods restarted, draining first while it works; a mode change during such a drain is refused. Compose, a machine over SSH and RunPod set no supervisor limit. The cluster acceptance check keeps its rig at 6 GiB by setting it headless, and checks the refusal in words for terminal. Tests pass on the Mac (121 in 18 files).

